---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { seo } from '../lib/content';
import { defaultSeo } from '../lib/blocks';
import { DOORS } from '../consts';

const door = DOORS[2];

// Editable from the joshpenzell.com admin; falls back to the default above.
const meta = await seo('ia.seo.consulting', defaultSeo('ia.seo.consulting'));
---

<Base
  title={meta.title}
  description={meta.description}
  path="/transformation-consulting/"
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Three ways to work together</p>
    <h1>Transformation consulting</h1>
    <p class="lede">{door.for}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap page-body">
    <p>
      <strong>Outcome.</strong>
      {door.outcome}
    </p>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Inspiration has a short half-life</h2>
      <p>
        A keynote can change what a room can see. It cannot change what happens on
        Thursday afternoon in a workflow nobody in the room owns. That gap is where most
        AI programs quietly stall—not because people resisted, but because nothing about
        the actual work was ever redesigned.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the engagement for organizations past the inspiration stage and willing to
        change behavior, workflow, learning, or operating practice.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Bounded experiments, not a transformation plan</h2>
      <p>
        We do not open with an eighteen-month roadmap. We open by finding the smallest
        real piece of work where a change would be visible, and running it somewhere the
        cost of being wrong is low.
      </p>
      <p>Each experiment is designed to produce three things:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Evidence</strong> — something observed, not asserted.</li>
        <li><strong>Trust</strong> — a team that has now done the thing once.</li>
        <li><strong>Direction</strong> — a clearer read on what should scale and what should not.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Scale follows evidence. That order is the whole method, and it is the part that is
        genuinely uncomfortable, because it means committing to fewer things earlier and
        finding out sooner when one of them is wrong.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Where the work usually lands</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Adoption that has plateaued after a tool rollout</li>
        <li>Learning and enablement programs measured by completion rather than by change</li>
        <li>Workflows where AI has been added beside the work instead of into it</li>
        <li>Teams competent in isolation and misaligned together</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="pull">Stop training people on AI long enough to build something with them.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-we-work/">See the method</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
